{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017npPghSnbuDPW63zxBUa7k","type":"tool_result","content":"70:func TestLoadDefaults(t *testing.T) {\n165:func TestLoadIntake(t *testing.T) {\n189:func TestLoadRedactGitleaks(t *testing.T) {\n211:func TestRoute(t *testing.T) {\n253:func TestLoadOverrides(t *testing.T) {\n317:func TestLoadPlanSection(t *testing.T) {\n332:func TestLoadErrors(t *testing.T) {\n783:func TestHostUntrusted(t *testing.T) {\n804:func TestRoleLookupHelpers(t *testing.T) {\n815:func TestErrorFormatting(t *testing.T) {\n832:func TestLoadProtectedRoundTrip(t *testing.T) {\n839:func TestSidecarSHARefAndFor(t *testing.T) {\n866:func TestWorkerNamesWithDigits(t *testing.T) {\n885:func TestLoadWhenKeepsDirSlash(t *testing.T) {\n893:func TestLoadWhenInvalidGlob(t *testing.T) {\n898:func TestLoadWhenEmptyRejected(t *testing.T) {\n909:func TestLLMScannerRules(t *testing.T) {\n9:func TestSourcePaths(t *testing.T) {\n16:func TestLocalPaused(t *testing.T) {\n258:sidecars:\n703:\t\t\tyaml: \"sidecars: {specs: {repo: a/b, url: x}}\\n\",\n704:\t\t\twant: []wantErr{{\".ard/config.yaml\", \"sidecars.specs.url\", \"unknown key\"}},\n708:\t\t\tyaml: \"sidecars: {specs: {repo: just-a-name}}\\n\",\n709:\t\t\twant: []wantErr{{\".ard/config.yaml\", \"sidecars.specs.repo\", `\"owner/name\"`}},\n97:`.ard/<issue#>/` is the only place agents leave durable output, and every file in it has one owner role. Issue directories are numeric, so they never collide with the role directories under `.ard/` (see Configuration).\n124:| a custom triage role | its role file only, unless `config.yaml` gives it `plan_section: <heading>` (see Configuration) |\n394:## Configuration\n396:Configuration is by convention: a role exists because its directory exists, and `.ard/config.yaml` holds only global settings and overrides. No prompt path is written anywhere; ard reads `.ard/<type>/<name>/prompt.txt`. Model credentials are host settings (`ARD_AGENT_AUTH`, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` in the `ard serve` environment), not `config.yaml` keys, because a PR can change `config.yaml`.\n472:- **Kill switch.** Four sources stop new claims, scanner runs (filing and autoclose), stale closes and merges: a local pause file `~/.ard/pause/<owner>/<repo>` outside the repository (the path is lowercased; `ard serve` creates the directory, so `touch` pauses and `rm` resumes), `paused: true` in the `config.yaml` in the host's working tree, `ard:pause` on the pinned status issue, and `paused: true` in the config on `origin/main`. They are checked cheapest first, before every claiming tick. The delay is at most one loop interval plus 10% jitter; a tick already running may finish its claims. `SIGUSR1` (Unix only) makes every loop run one tick now, logged as `controller.wake`; the tick still passes the kill switch and the job pool, and wakes that arrive while a tick runs coalesce into one extra tick. The gate answers one controller-wide question and has no per-kind checks, so every loop that writes (scanner filing and autoclose, stale closes, merges, claims), except the Reaper, the cost-table writer and in-flight close-outs, must report `Claims() == true` and is skipped while paused. Running jobs, in-flight close-outs, the Reaper and the `costs` loop continue: the table is bookkeeping about work already done, not new work. The check fails closed: an unreadable status issue, a failed fetch of `origin/main`, or a config that fails to load counts as paused. The pause file pauses when anything exists at the path (a file, a directory or a symlink; content is ignored), and a path that cannot be examined, for example because `pause/<owner>` is a regular file, also counts as paused. The local working-tree `config.yaml` is read only for its `paused` key; a missing, unreadable or invalid file does not pause by itself, but a readable one that says `paused: true` does. The pause file and the local `paused` are the kill switches that nothing pushed to GitHub can lift. Config and role directories otherwise come from `origin/main` at the same SHA the tick's jobs use (see Configuration). `ard serve` remembers the status issue number in `_controller.db` (`meta` key `status_issue`). At startup it reads that issue directly and adopts it if it is still open, titled `ard status`, carries the `<!-- ard:status -->` marker and has the App as author; otherwise it searches by those same tests, and creates and pins the issue if none matches. Search is eventually consistent, so the remembered number is what stops a quick restart from creating a second issue; if two still match, the lowest number wins. A cached issue that cannot be read for a reason other than not-found falls back to search rather than aborting startup. A dry-run never stores its synthetic (negative) number; a real number found by search is stored only in the separate dry-run database. A second `ard serve` on the same repo exits with an error: the leader lock is an `flock` on `~/.ard/locks/<owner>/<repo>.lock` (local filesystem only), shared by live and dry-run.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ca5cf25b-3e4f-4ffa-a661-8e00b2ab0e60","uuid":"bb37c068-1317-4d15-9562-8029252146de","timestamp":"2026-11-24T12:03:56.854Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"70:func TestLoadDefaults(t *testing.T) {\n165:func TestLoadIntake(t *testing.T) {\n189:func TestLoadRedactGitleaks(t *testing.T) {\n211:func TestRoute(t *testing.T) {\n253:func TestLoadOverrides(t *testing.T) {\n317:func TestLoadPlanSection(t *testing.T) {\n332:func TestLoadErrors(t *testing.T) {\n783:func TestHostUntrusted(t *testing.T) {\n804:func TestRoleLookupHelpers(t *testing.T) {\n815:func TestErrorFormatting(t *testing.T) {\n832:func TestLoadProtectedRoundTrip(t *testing.T) {\n839:func TestSidecarSHARefAndFor(t *testing.T) {\n866:func TestWorkerNamesWithDigits(t *testing.T) {\n885:func TestLoadWhenKeepsDirSlash(t *testing.T) {\n893:func TestLoadWhenInvalidGlob(t *testing.T) {\n898:fu